 summary: [
  ["Three kinds of substances", "Substances around us may be classified as acidic, basic or neutral. Neutral substances, like sugar solution, salt solution and tap water, are neither acidic nor basic."],
  ["Acid–base indicators", "Substances that show different colours in acidic and basic solutions are called acid–base indicators. Extracts of lichen (litmus), red rose, red hibiscus, purple cabbage and turmeric are natural indicators."],
  ["Litmus", "Litmus is obtained from lichens. Acids turn blue litmus red; bases turn red litmus blue. Neutral substances change neither."],
  ["Taste and touch", "Acidic substances like lemon, amla, tamarind and vinegar taste sour (citric, ascorbic, tartaric, acetic acids). Bases feel slippery and generally taste bitter. Never taste unknown substances."],
  ["Red rose extract", "Red rose extract gives a red colour in acidic solutions and a green colour in basic solutions, and stays unchanged in neutral ones."],
  ["Turmeric", "Turmeric turns red in basic solutions but stays yellow in acidic and neutral solutions, so it can test only for bases."],
  ["Olfactory indicators", "Some substances, like onion, change their odour in acidic or basic media. They are olfactory indicators and can be used even without seeing colours."],
  ["Neutralisation", "An acid and a base neutralise each other: Acid + Base → Salt + Water + Heat. The resulting solution is neither acidic nor basic."],
  ["Neutralisation in daily life", "Moist baking soda soothes an ant bite (formic acid); lime treats acidic soil and manure or compost treats basic soil; acidic factory waste is neutralised with bases before release to protect fish."]
 ]
